**Response to Weakness 3 (No comparison with frontier models ( GPT-4, Claude, or Gemini), even on a subset would have being an interesting analysis.):**

We appreciate this valuable comment.

As mentioned, our focus was on ensuring evaluation stability and reproducibility. To reduce LLM output variability, each experiment used around 9,000 samples with extensive personalization trials. Although we acknowledge the value of benchmarking against frontier models,API costs make large-scale personalized evaluations infeasible. Our study therefore prioritizes an open, reproducible, model-agnostic evaluation framework, which can accommodate such comparisons in the future.

**Response to Question 1 ( how many actual dialogues and turns have being generated?.):**
Thank you for your question.

We generated 46,113 samples, each consisting of:
     a persona;
     a single instance of an explicit preference and its related utterance;
     a dialogue from which the explicit preference can be inferred.;

Each dialogue contains 6-10 turns. We observed that dialogues longer than 10 turns tend to show much noticeable deviations from realistic child–LLM interactions and may negatively affect the coherence and interpretability of the generated outputs. Limiting dialogue length in this way allows us to maintain a balance between generating sufficiently rich interactions and preserving output quality, while keeping the evaluation reproducible and reliable.

Here is an example from the samples, in this example there are 7 turns in the implicit_dialogue.
```xml
<example>
<persona>A 4-year-old child with a strong interest in language and expression. He enjoys mimicking the tone and intonation of adults, and although he sometimes makes people laugh due to inaccurate pronunciation, this does not hinder his enjoyment of expressing himself. Both of his parents come from bilingual families, so he naturally learned two languages in his daily life and can use them for basic communication. He is curious about books and stories, enjoys listening to various interesting stories told by his parents, and tries to express them in his own words. He also likes to mimic animal sounds, especially those of kittens and puppies, and he will laugh happily whenever he hears these sounds. His sensitive personality makes him a bit shy when encountering strangers, but he quickly adapts to new environments and uses language and expression to connect with others.</persona>

<explicit_preference>I don't like sticky things; it feels so uncomfortable when they get on my hands, especially when I'm playing outside.</explicit_preference>
<related_utterance>Mom said she's going to take me on a picnic this weekend, have you ever been on a picnic?</related_utterance>

<implicit_dialogue>
{'role': 'child', 'content': "Mom said she's going to take me to the park this weekend, can I bring some snacks?"}
{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Of course you can! What yummy food do you want to bring?'}
{'role': 'child', 'content': "I want to bring some cookies and fruits, but I don't like sticky things; it feels uncomfortable when they get on my hands."}
{'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Got it, then let's bring some dry cookies and fruits so your hands won't get sticky. What kind of cookies do you like the most?"}
{'role': 'user', 'content': "I like chocolate cookies, they are sweet and don't make my hands sticky."}
{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Chocolate cookies sound delicious! What fruits do you plan to bring?'}
{'role': 'user', 'content': "I want to bring apples and bananas, but banana peels are a bit sticky, so I'll just bring apples."}
{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Apples are a great choice, they are clean and easy to eat. What else do you plan to bring to the park?'}
{'role': 'user', 'content': "I also want to bring a small mat, so I won't touch the wet grass when I sit down."}
{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'A small mat is a good idea, so you can sit comfortably. What do you plan to do at the park?'}
{'role': 'user', 'content': 'I want to run and jump, and bark like a dog, woof! Woof woof!'}
{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Ha ha, just like a real dog! Do you mimic other animals too?'}
{'role': 'user', 'content': 'I can also mimic the sound of a kitten, meow~ Sometimes I even tell stories to myself.'}
{'role': 'assistant', 'content': "That's awesome! I bet your stories are very interesting. What story will you tell at the park?"}
</implicit_dialogue>
</example>
